+# Accepted lexical bridge source milestone
+
+The source-only replay passed. The fixed decoded JSON number program now has a kernel-checked connection to the existing independent numeric lexical reference on normalized ASCII number-entry inputs. It preserves exact raw consumed prefix and remainder, all three digit runs, outer-sign presence and syntactic integer classification. This closes a Lean-model connection; it is not a native Rust or floating-point refinement result.
+
+| Gate | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Fresh copied source modules | 60 preserved + 11 new |
+| Named theorem axiom audits | 457: 398 preserved + 59 new |
+| New audited statements | 37 general laws + 22 boundary cases |
+| Actual fixed JSON-file rows | 62: 48 admitted observations + 14 domain refusals |
+| Independent lexical reference rows | Same 62 rows and expected results |
+| Changed valid JSON-file rows | 2; supplied program changes `1+2` behavior |
+| Malformed actual-file documents | 5 exact refusals |
+| Semantic adapter mutants | 3 compile, then fail unchanged laws and exact false witnesses |
+| Preserved-source drift controls | 3 exact refusals |
+| Immutable source authentication | 63 files matched signed Git objects |
+| Compiled scratch | Removed after receipts |
+
+The generic chain and limits are in [MAPPING.md](MAPPING.md). The public caller premise is `NormalizedASCII source ∧ NumericEntry.nativeStart source = true`. Mathematical helper laws lift every byte to its equally numbered scalar; they do not claim to decode arbitrary UTF-8. Both initial model-error values are preserved, without mapping that Boolean to native error state. Numeric-core remainder and full-token remainder are deliberately distinct and separately checked.
+
+The actual-file runner executes the parser returned by the existing typed JSON decoder. It imports no fixed fixture or correspondence module. The kernel theorem uses the accepted quoted `Lean.Json` fixture, whose literal is byte-bound to the actual file by the checker. The separate changed-valid fixture proves runtime dependence on the supplied file, rather than a fixed-profile substitution. JSON text parsing and quotation remain trusted boundaries.
+
+The numeric projection `NumberTransfer.project`, its exact significand/scale semantics and native floating-point rounding are unchanged and outside the new bridge. CSS prose interpretation is still manually mapped. No new CSS feature-coverage claim, native execution, canonical edit, browser run, WPT change or Git mutation occurred.
+
+Source provenance uses canonical commit `b3d6ef2c643b848851d5e860c836abf2120e56af` and the earlier lexical model commit `5a9dc434eff56b9f65b27f1d2ae8ce35251b38f2`. [PRESERVED.json](PRESERVED.json) records exact paths/hashes, and [ORIGINS.json](ORIGINS.json) records the author's read-only Git object authentication. Older `NumericDigits`, `Name` and `SemanticName` produce nine known unused-simp style warnings under the pinned toolchain. The checker accepts only their exact full-output hashes, preserves complete logs, and applies strict diagnostics to all other builds; no warnings are globally suppressed.
+
+Reproduction:
+
+```sh
+python3 -B artifacts/css-number-lexical-bridge-20261110-v1/replay.py --lean artifacts/stylo-bloom-tools/lean-4.35.0-rc4-darwin_aarch64/bin/lean --out /tmp/css-number-lexical-replay
+```
